        "## 10. Findings and recommendations\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question 1 - Parental education.** Global scores are higher on average among students with more educated parents. A father with postgraduate education is associated with a mean of 177.0, compared with 155.2 for completed primary schooling; the corresponding mother means are 175.6 and 155.3. Each parent's education accounts for about 8% of unadjusted global-score variation, and the two shares overlap. Model A's contrast for two university-educated parents versus two parents with primary schooling or less is 18.3 points. After socioeconomic controls and the student's Saber 11 average are included, the mother coefficient is +0.71 (95% CI -0.48 to 1.90, p=.241) and the father coefficient is -0.13 (95% CI -1.20 to 0.95, p=.820). These estimates are much smaller and their intervals include zero. This attenuation does not establish a causal pathway or rule out disadvantages during university.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question 2 - Socioeconomic stratum.** Stratum 6 has the highest mean global score (181.5) and Stratum 1 the lowest (152.4), a 29.2-point gap. All 15 pairwise Tukey comparisons are significant under the test's assumptions. English has the largest standardized Stratum 6-1 difference among the recorded domains at each assessment. Among Saber Pro domains, written communication has the smallest difference (d=0.56); quantitative reasoning is d=0.76. The stratum gap is approximately 1.5 SD on the high-school average and 1.3 SD on the global score. This is a comparison of relative positions within this linked sample, not a measure of learning gains or the causal effect of the degree. In Model C, the Stratum 6-1 coefficient is +0.37 (95% CI -1.45 to 2.18, p=.692). This particular model adjusts for parental education, gender, school sector, internet and the high-school average.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question 3 - Gender.** Men average 163.7 and women 161.3 on the global score, an unadjusted difference of 2.4 points (95% CI 1.6 to 3.2; d=0.105). Quantitative reasoning favors men (d=0.42); written communication favors women (d=-0.16). The other three Saber Pro domains differ by less than 0.07 SD. Models B and C use the same complete-case sample: the male-female coefficient changes from +2.08 to -0.78 when the high-school average is added. In Model C the reversal is small but statistically significant under conventional OLS errors (95% CI -1.32 to -0.24, p=.005). It should not be described as a difference that disappears, and the model does not identify when or why it arose. Female representation differs by program: approximately 12% in mechanical, 20% in electronic and 22% in electric engineering, versus 52% in industrial and 55% in chemical engineering.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question 4 - Programs and other findings.** The high-school average correlates r=.78 with the global score. A model with all five high-school subjects accounts for about 61% of global-score variation. Chemical engineering has both the highest raw mean (176.7) and the largest mean residual (+3.3 points); electric engineering's residual is +1.0. Civil, industrial, electronic and mechanical engineering have residual means of +0.2, -0.5, -0.9 and -1.2. These estimates describe departures from a fitted baseline; they do not rank teaching quality. Students not classified by SISBEN average 167.5 versus 152.7 in Level 1. The unclassified category does not show eligibility for aid or grant receipt. Private-school graduates and students with home internet also have higher raw means. Written communication has the lowest overall numerical mean among the five Saber Pro domains (53.7), but means alone cannot establish comparable proficiency or instructional responsiveness across tests.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Recommendations.**\n",
        "1. Pilot support at university entry using individual diagnostics. Use stratum, parental education, school sector and access information to guide outreach, while allowing students from any group to participate when they need help.\n",
        "2. Evaluate English support for students with low diagnostic scores, particularly those from lower strata, given the observed English gaps. Measure learning and costs rather than assuming a benefit from these associations.\n",
        "3. Pilot writing support in mechanical and electronic engineering, where mean written-communication values are 50.5 and 51.2. Civil engineering also merits attention (51.3), and industrial engineering reaches the largest group (n=5,318; writing mean 54.1). Electric engineering can provide a further pilot context (57.8), but high male representation alone does not establish greater need or treatment benefit. Chemical engineering's higher writing mean (64.0) is a reason to study its practices, not proof that those practices caused the result.\n",
        "4. Report raw outcomes alongside adjusted descriptive comparisons and sample sizes. Consider additional baseline and institutional variables before using residuals in consequential program evaluations.\n",
